1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:02,890
Restore is a comforting word.

2
00:00:03,450 --> 00:00:05,590
It doesn't tell you which state you're getting
back.

3
00:00:06,390 --> 00:00:08,770
In this lesson, we'll inspect a real Agent

4
00:00:08,770 --> 00:00:12,230
experiment, restore its checkpoint, and
compare the result

5
00:00:12,230 --> 00:00:13,930
with a targeted Git recovery.

6
00:00:14,610 --> 00:00:17,190
We'll track the code, a manual note, and

7
00:00:17,190 --> 00:00:19,570
a tiny file created by a terminal command.

8
00:00:20,150 --> 00:00:22,770
The useful skill is explaining what changed,
what

9
00:00:22,770 --> 00:00:24,550
survived, and how you checked it.

10
00:00:26,750 --> 00:00:30,230
Start by naming the state. Cursor checkpoints
support

11
00:00:30,230 --> 00:00:31,750
local Agent exploration.

12
00:00:32,670 --> 00:00:35,950
Git gives us committed snapshots we can
identify and compare.

13
00:00:37,030 --> 00:00:39,730
Commands can also affect files and services
beyond the

14
00:00:39,730 --> 00:00:40,610
edit we're reviewing.

15
00:00:41,130 --> 00:00:42,510
Those are separate things to check.

16
00:00:43,210 --> 00:00:46,610
Before recovering anything, inspect the
current diff and

17
00:00:46,610 --> 00:00:48,250
preserve work you may want later.

18
00:00:48,890 --> 00:00:51,310
Then choose the operation that matches the
state

19
00:00:51,310 --> 00:00:52,990
you actually intend to restore.

20
00:00:55,483 --> 00:00:57,763
Here's our dedicated recovery checkout.

21
00:00:58,543 --> 00:01:01,003
I'm adding a manual note before asking Agent

22
00:01:01,003 --> 00:01:01,963
to change the code.

23
00:01:02,523 --> 00:01:04,823
The sentence gives us something concrete to
track.

24
00:01:05,383 --> 00:01:07,383
Preserve this sentence during the experiment.

25
00:01:08,003 --> 00:01:09,843
It lives in a different file from the

26
00:01:09,843 --> 00:01:11,043
implementation and tests.

27
00:01:11,723 --> 00:01:14,243
That distinction matters because we want to
observe

28
00:01:14,243 --> 00:01:17,003
the recovery boundary instead of assuming that
every

29
00:01:17,003 --> 00:01:19,343
file in the workspace will behave the same
way.

30
00:01:21,817 --> 00:01:24,737
The terminal shows our baseline commit and the

31
00:01:24,737 --> 00:01:26,157
current working tree status.

32
00:01:26,677 --> 00:01:27,997
Only the note is modified.

33
00:01:28,617 --> 00:01:30,897
Its diff contains the sentence we just wrote.

34
00:01:31,477 --> 00:01:34,297
The implementation and tests still match the
committed

35
00:01:34,297 --> 00:01:36,777
starting point and the targeted checks pass.

36
00:01:37,437 --> 00:01:38,697
Save that starting evidence.

37
00:01:39,397 --> 00:01:42,197
After the experiment, three modified files
will have

38
00:01:42,197 --> 00:01:42,997
different origins.

39
00:01:43,677 --> 00:01:46,077
One edit was already ours, while two files

40
00:01:46,077 --> 00:01:47,497
will contain the Agent's changes.

41
00:01:51,283 --> 00:01:54,143
Ask Agent to explore one display change.

42
00:01:54,823 --> 00:01:57,883
Open requests should say Needs review instead
of

43
00:01:57,883 --> 00:01:58,823
Awaiting review.

44
00:01:59,623 --> 00:02:02,723
Update the contract comment and matching test
expectations,

45
00:02:03,083 --> 00:02:05,603
preserving all nine tests and the other
behavior.

46
00:02:06,303 --> 00:02:07,983
Keep the manual note untouched.

47
00:02:08,743 --> 00:02:10,703
We also ask for a tiny marker file

48
00:02:10,703 --> 00:02:12,823
through the terminal outside the repository.

49
00:02:13,463 --> 00:02:15,763
That marker will help us check whether a

50
00:02:15,763 --> 00:02:20,003
file recovery operation also reverses this
particular command's effect.

51
00:02:22,483 --> 00:02:25,583
The real Agent reads the current work, edits

52
00:02:25,583 --> 00:02:28,503
the label and its expectations, then runs the

53
00:02:28,503 --> 00:02:30,023
focused tests and type check.

54
00:02:30,563 --> 00:02:33,083
It also creates the marker through a terminal
command.

55
00:02:33,723 --> 00:02:35,523
Watch those as distinct actions.

56
00:02:36,163 --> 00:02:38,343
Changing source code and running a command can

57
00:02:38,343 --> 00:02:41,023
both happen in one conversation, but they
don't

58
00:02:41,023 --> 00:02:43,223
automatically share one recovery mechanism.

59
00:02:43,643 --> 00:02:46,423
The task stays inside our isolated course
environment

60
00:02:46,423 --> 00:02:49,263
with no database or remote changes requested.

61
00:02:51,617 --> 00:02:55,477
The implementation diff changes the contract
comment and

62
00:02:55,477 --> 00:02:57,177
the exact open label branch.

63
00:02:57,897 --> 00:03:01,257
The unknown value fallback, batch mapping, and
permission

64
00:03:01,257 --> 00:03:02,737
helper remain unchanged.

65
00:03:03,657 --> 00:03:05,957
The Agent reports nine passing tests.

66
00:03:06,577 --> 00:03:09,537
That's useful evidence for this experiment,
but green

67
00:03:09,537 --> 00:03:12,397
tests don't decide whether we want to keep the
experiment.

68
00:03:13,037 --> 00:03:15,957
The expected label now matches the experiment.

69
00:03:16,497 --> 00:03:18,597
The test suite agrees with the new wording.

70
00:03:21,150 --> 00:03:24,170
Look at the expected values in the batch
tests.

71
00:03:24,970 --> 00:03:28,290
Mixed inputs, duplicates, and the frozen array
result

72
00:03:28,290 --> 00:03:31,930
now use Needs review where an open status
appears.

73
00:03:32,770 --> 00:03:35,690
Unknown values and rejected labels still have
their

74
00:03:35,690 --> 00:03:39,130
existing expectations and empty input stays
empty.

75
00:03:39,850 --> 00:03:41,890
Both files describe the same experiment.

76
00:03:42,650 --> 00:03:46,390
Before recovery, we've preserved the actual
diff, so

77
00:03:46,390 --> 00:03:50,470
the experiment remains inspectable even after
its files change again.

78
00:03:52,917 --> 00:03:55,977
The curved arrow attached to our request opens

79
00:03:55,977 --> 00:03:57,237
the checkpoint confirmation.

80
00:03:58,097 --> 00:04:00,637
In this version, the dialog asks whether to

81
00:04:00,637 --> 00:04:02,777
discard changes up to that checkpoint.

82
00:04:03,497 --> 00:04:05,977
Read the control you're actually using and
keep

83
00:04:05,977 --> 00:04:08,137
the current diff somewhere you can inspect
later.

84
00:04:08,937 --> 00:04:11,617
We leave the confirmation enabled and continue
with

85
00:04:11,617 --> 00:04:12,897
this isolated experiment.

86
00:04:13,417 --> 00:04:15,437
A familiar icon is a starting point for

87
00:04:15,437 --> 00:04:18,417
inspection, not a substitute for understanding
the scope.

88
00:04:20,683 --> 00:04:23,823
The open label is back to Awaiting review

89
00:04:23,823 --> 00:04:26,323
and the experimental highlights disappear.

90
00:04:27,323 --> 00:04:30,483
Notice that the conversation still contains
the earlier report.

91
00:04:31,103 --> 00:04:34,343
That report describes the experiment before we
restored it.

92
00:04:34,703 --> 00:04:36,723
It is not a live statement of the

93
00:04:36,723 --> 00:04:37,723
current working tree.

94
00:04:38,443 --> 00:04:39,743
This is an easy trap.

95
00:04:40,303 --> 00:04:43,103
The files changed while the written
explanation stayed.

96
00:04:43,843 --> 00:04:46,383
After recovery, read the actual state again.

97
00:04:48,850 --> 00:04:52,410
Run Git status and inspect the note. Only

98
00:04:52,410 --> 00:04:55,350
the manual note remains modified and its
sentence

99
00:04:55,350 --> 00:04:58,450
is still there. The implementation and test
files

100
00:04:58,450 --> 00:05:01,670
match their contents before the Agent request.
The

101
00:05:01,670 --> 00:05:05,110
baseline commit is unchanged, too. In this
experiment,

102
00:05:05,110 --> 00:05:08,250
the checkpoint recovered the Agent's file
edits while

103
00:05:08,250 --> 00:05:11,110
preserving the note that already existed. Keep
that

104
00:05:11,110 --> 00:05:13,550
claim specific to the state we established and

105
00:05:13,550 --> 00:05:14,650
the result we verified.

106
00:05:17,117 --> 00:05:19,897
Now read the marker from the temporary
directory.

107
00:05:20,537 --> 00:05:21,217
It's still there.

108
00:05:21,857 --> 00:05:24,817
The terminal command created that file outside
our

109
00:05:24,817 --> 00:05:28,417
repository and restoring this checkpoint did
not remove it.

110
00:05:28,817 --> 00:05:30,777
That's a concrete boundary we can observe.

111
00:05:31,437 --> 00:05:34,297
We haven't tested every possible command or
service.

112
00:05:34,857 --> 00:05:37,717
The lesson is to check external effects
independently.

113
00:05:38,577 --> 00:05:41,237
A source file returning to an earlier version

114
00:05:41,237 --> 00:05:44,277
does not prove that a process, database, or

115
00:05:44,277 --> 00:05:45,917
remote operation was reversed.

116
00:05:48,483 --> 00:05:51,803
Run the status tests again, followed by the
type check.

117
00:05:52,563 --> 00:05:55,123
All nine tests pass on the recovered files.

118
00:05:55,863 --> 00:05:58,483
We also compared the actual contents with the

119
00:05:58,483 --> 00:06:00,223
saved state from before the request.

120
00:06:01,103 --> 00:06:03,023
Those checks answer different questions.

121
00:06:03,463 --> 00:06:06,363
The comparison tells us which files returned,
while

122
00:06:06,363 --> 00:06:08,423
the tests check their expected behavior.

123
00:06:09,203 --> 00:06:12,023
The preserved manual note and the remaining
marker

124
00:06:12,023 --> 00:06:14,383
are part of the outcome, not clutter to

125
00:06:14,383 --> 00:06:15,343
hide from the result.

126
00:06:17,783 --> 00:06:21,343
For the Git comparison, make a deliberate
manual edit.

127
00:06:21,863 --> 00:06:24,583
Change the unknown value fallback to No label,

128
00:06:24,883 --> 00:06:27,163
leaving the comment and tests unchanged.

129
00:06:27,863 --> 00:06:30,623
This is a prepared mistake in our disposable
checkout.

130
00:06:31,463 --> 00:06:34,763
The earlier checkpoint experiment is already
undone.

131
00:06:35,323 --> 00:06:37,463
Now the source has a new manual change,

132
00:06:37,883 --> 00:06:40,203
and the note still has its earlier sentence.

133
00:06:40,823 --> 00:06:43,543
We want to recover this one implementation
file

134
00:06:43,543 --> 00:06:44,723
from the committed baseline.

135
00:06:47,150 --> 00:06:50,870
The focused diff shows exactly the fallback
replacement.

136
00:06:51,570 --> 00:06:55,490
Our tests still expect Unknown status for
unrecognized values.

137
00:06:56,250 --> 00:06:59,270
Before restoring the file, preserve the
current changes

138
00:06:59,270 --> 00:07:01,090
in a patch outside the checkout.

139
00:07:01,970 --> 00:07:04,830
Git restore will replace contents in the path
we select.

140
00:07:05,630 --> 00:07:07,510
Naming a source and a path makes the

141
00:07:07,510 --> 00:07:10,050
operation easier to review, but it doesn't
make

142
00:07:10,050 --> 00:07:11,770
the current contents worth forgetting.

143
00:07:14,283 --> 00:07:17,743
Run the full status test file before
recovering anything.

144
00:07:18,623 --> 00:07:20,423
Six tests pass and three fail.

145
00:07:21,143 --> 00:07:24,463
The summary identifies the affected
expectations and the

146
00:07:24,463 --> 00:07:26,703
first failure shows the mismatch directly.

147
00:07:27,663 --> 00:07:28,503
Actual: No label.

148
00:07:28,983 --> 00:07:30,543
Expected: Unknown status.

149
00:07:31,223 --> 00:07:33,903
We have a specific observed failure caused by

150
00:07:33,903 --> 00:07:34,903
our manual change.

151
00:07:35,663 --> 00:07:37,523
Keep the expectations intact.

152
00:07:38,183 --> 00:07:41,323
We're recovering the implementation to the
committed contract,

153
00:07:41,323 --> 00:07:44,803
so rewriting the tests would answer a
different question.

154
00:07:47,250 --> 00:07:49,450
Read the command before running it.

155
00:07:49,970 --> 00:07:51,990
The source is HEAD, our current commit.

156
00:07:52,570 --> 00:07:55,130
The destination is the working tree, and the

157
00:07:55,130 --> 00:07:57,310
path is only the status implementation.

158
00:07:57,950 --> 00:07:59,590
The staging area is left alone.

159
00:08:00,230 --> 00:08:02,830
Now run the restore and inspect Git status.

160
00:08:03,570 --> 00:08:06,030
Only the separate manual note remains
modified.

161
00:08:06,710 --> 00:08:08,730
The focused source diff is empty.

162
00:08:09,310 --> 00:08:11,790
We also compare the saved file contents and

163
00:08:11,790 --> 00:08:14,610
revision independently so we can explain the
state

164
00:08:14,610 --> 00:08:15,730
we actually recovered.

165
00:08:18,217 --> 00:08:19,557
Read the note again.

166
00:08:20,137 --> 00:08:21,557
The earlier sentence remains.

167
00:08:22,377 --> 00:08:24,997
Now rerun the status suite and the type check.

168
00:08:25,657 --> 00:08:28,217
All nine tests pass, and the type check

169
00:08:28,217 --> 00:08:29,237
completes successfully.

170
00:08:29,957 --> 00:08:31,837
The source matches our committed baseline.

171
00:08:32,397 --> 00:08:34,917
The tests were never rewritten for this manual
mistake.

172
00:08:35,717 --> 00:08:37,337
That gives us a useful sequence.

173
00:08:38,157 --> 00:08:40,917
Inspect the change, observe the failure,
preserve the

174
00:08:40,917 --> 00:08:44,557
patch, recover one path, and verify the
resulting behavior.

175
00:08:44,557 --> 00:08:47,657
The command is only one step in that sequence.

176
00:08:50,217 --> 00:08:54,117
This original model separates the committed
tree, staged

177
00:08:54,117 --> 00:08:55,897
contents, and working files.

178
00:08:56,597 --> 00:08:59,997
The example gives the implementation a
different value in each.

179
00:09:00,777 --> 00:09:03,197
Choose the committed tree as the source, the

180
00:09:03,197 --> 00:09:06,057
working tree as the destination, and only the

181
00:09:06,057 --> 00:09:07,157
implementation path.

182
00:09:07,777 --> 00:09:08,777
Apply it to the model.

183
00:09:09,517 --> 00:09:12,077
The working value returns to Awaiting review.

184
00:09:12,737 --> 00:09:15,677
The staged value and the manual note stay as
they were.

185
00:09:16,217 --> 00:09:18,757
No command executes in your repository here.

186
00:09:21,183 --> 00:09:24,563
Reset the example and choose the index as the
source.

187
00:09:25,383 --> 00:09:28,143
Restoring only the working file now copies the

188
00:09:28,143 --> 00:09:31,923
staged value, Needs review, instead of the
committed value.

189
00:09:32,463 --> 00:09:34,523
That's why the source matters when the index

190
00:09:34,523 --> 00:09:35,543
differs from the commit.

191
00:09:36,143 --> 00:09:39,203
You can also explore explicit staging
destinations with

192
00:09:39,203 --> 00:09:40,023
the committed source.

193
00:09:41,023 --> 00:09:44,483
Predict the affected cells first, then compare
the result.

194
00:09:45,083 --> 00:09:48,503
This simplified model covers tracked files not
every

195
00:09:48,503 --> 00:09:50,083
situation Git can represent.

196
00:09:52,650 --> 00:09:54,650
Save a short recovery record.

197
00:09:55,250 --> 00:09:58,410
Identify the starting revision, the operation,
its intended

198
00:09:58,410 --> 00:10:00,550
scope, and what you actually observed.

199
00:10:01,230 --> 00:10:03,890
For our checkpoint experiment, the code and
tests

200
00:10:03,890 --> 00:10:06,950
returned, the manual note remained, and the
terminal

201
00:10:06,950 --> 00:10:08,090
marker still existed.

202
00:10:08,650 --> 00:10:11,850
We checked the files, Git state, and tests
separately.

203
00:10:12,470 --> 00:10:15,530
A useful record connects the recovery action
to

204
00:10:15,530 --> 00:10:18,930
evidence, including state that didn't change
and limits

205
00:10:18,930 --> 00:10:19,790
we haven't tested.

206
00:10:22,350 --> 00:10:26,090
Clone the supplied practice snapshot and
record its baseline.

207
00:10:26,610 --> 00:10:28,010
Make two separate edits.

208
00:10:28,350 --> 00:10:30,430
Change a display label and add a note.

209
00:10:31,070 --> 00:10:34,290
Inspect the diff and preserve a patch outside
the checkout.

210
00:10:34,890 --> 00:10:38,170
Then restore only the implementation from the
committed tree.

211
00:10:38,810 --> 00:10:41,530
Verify that your note remains and rerun the
checks.

212
00:10:42,370 --> 00:10:46,510
Explain the source, destination, and path
before pressing Enter.

213
00:10:47,070 --> 00:10:49,670
The exercise is complete when the resulting
state

214
00:10:49,670 --> 00:10:50,710
matches your prediction.

215
00:10:53,317 --> 00:10:56,157
You now have two recovery routes you can
explain.

216
00:10:56,717 --> 00:10:59,437
A checkpoint can help with local Agent
exploration.

217
00:11:00,437 --> 00:11:03,157
Git can restore named paths from a committed
state.

218
00:11:03,937 --> 00:11:06,737
Neither a familiar button nor an old
conversation

219
00:11:06,737 --> 00:11:08,537
report proves the current result.

220
00:11:09,277 --> 00:11:10,777
Inspect before and after.

221
00:11:11,217 --> 00:11:13,057
Keep the work you may need, and check

222
00:11:13,057 --> 00:11:14,437
external effects separately.

223
00:11:14,957 --> 00:11:16,857
Before the next lesson, name one piece of

224
00:11:16,857 --> 00:11:20,017
state you'd verify independently after
restoring an Agent experiment.
